Wall scattering, accommodation and molecular memory (@gas-wall-scattering: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @industrial-gases-and-vacuum, #source "../sources/Outgassing-Properties-Chiggiato.lingua#r_18MM2A316NWRJABS8E53468PWT", #source "../sources/Fundamentals-of-Vacuum-Technology-Leybold.lingua#r_1S93A5X32978GSHZH4CF96VPQR", #industrial-gases-and-vacuum "Thin-aperture-conductance-and-two-way-transfer.lingua#r_4JKVFE59JBAXFV1AW2E9EXH73G") { r_2RRHVCRVY3A76AQECF8306F12J
**Read:** [Outgassing properties of vacuum materials — Paolo Chiggiato](../sources/Outgassing-Properties-Chiggiato.lingua#r_18MM2A316NWRJABS8E53468PWT), adsorption/desorption and surface history; [Fundamentals of Vacuum Technology — Walter Umrath and contributors](../sources/Fundamentals-of-Vacuum-Technology-Leybold.lingua#r_1S93A5X32978GSHZH4CF96VPQR), molecular flow. Use the shared thermal interface material for energy accommodation.

A molecule reaching a wall may reflect, exchange energy, adsorb for a time, react or later desorb. Specular reflection preserves a geometric relation to the incident direction. A diffuse equilibrium re-emission model uses a cosine angular distribution relative to the surface normal and a wall-temperature-dependent speed distribution.

A diffuse direction model and complete thermal accommodation are separate assumptions. A coated, contaminated, cryogenic or reactive surface can behave differently from a clean room-temperature wall. If residence times are long, geometry alone does not determine a transient transmission response.

**Work:** Derive a normalized cosine angular distribution over a hemisphere. Compare specular and diffuse trajectories through a bent passage. Identify which assumptions are needed to turn a geometric transmission probability into a conductance. Explain what a pumping or surface-history observation could reveal about an otherwise invisible wall reservoir.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
